  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 148 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nThrough Friday: Continued colder than normal and dry. Latest \nsatellite imagery/RAP analysis showed the circulation around a \nclosed low spinning over northern Alabama and central Tennessee. \nThere was still plenty of stratus across our northeast associated \nwith this feature. The closed low will continue to lift out to the \nnortheast through Friday and the stratus will continue to erode from \nthe southeast as it does so. Eventhough the closed low will be well \nto our northeast Friday broad troughing aloft will linger over our \nregion. Latest surface analysis had an 1025mb low centered over \nsouthern Texas. This low will shift east and off the Texas coast by \nFriday afternoon. This will help maintain a west low level flow \nbeneath the west northwest flow aloft and lead to another cooler \nthan normal day Friday. Coldest morning lows this morning were in \nthe west. Coldest morning lows Friday morning will be in our \nnortheast where temperatures will bottom out below freeing. /22/\n\nFriday Night into mid week...\n\nTemperatures stay chilly under the influence of high pressure \nthrough Friday night with another round of near to below freezing \nlows expected along and east of I-55 with middle 30s F lows to the \nwest. \n\nA noticeable rebound in temperatures comes Saturday as winds begin \nto shift to southerly return flow with the high pressure center \nshifting east. Highs Saturday rise into the 60s F across the area \nwith a chance for near 70 F highs west of the Mississippi River. A \nrather dramatic difference in lows Saturday night is expected as \ndeep return flow and moisture/clouds builds in west of I-55 where \nlows in the mid to upper 50s F are likely compared to areas to the \neast that may dip into the mid to lower 40s F. \n\nThe next front will approach early Sunday as a short wave upper \ntrough and attendant surface low approaches from the west. This \nsystem appears likely to bring an organized cyclone into the area \nalong a track that would often portend severe weather. However, the \ncool, dry continental air that we will have entrenched over the next \nfew days is slow to retreat and the timing of the front likely being \nin the cooler morning hours for most of our area suggests that the \nsystem will be substantially limited in its ability to produce \nsustained, organized, deep thunderstorm cores. This narrow, pinched \nwarm sector will likely limit mixed layer cape to sub-500 J/KG \nvalues even in the more moisture rich environment across our far \nsouthern counties. For this reason, no areas were highlighted for \nsevere thunderstorm risk this forecast cycle. Should model guidance \ntrend a bit more aggressive with the moisture return and/or the \nsystem appear to be a bit slower than currently estimated, this \ncould lead to a lower end (Marginal) severe weather risk for areas \nsouth of I-55 as we get closer to the weekend. Otherwise, a few \nthunderstorms and briefly heavy rain is possible across the area. \n\nStorm activity clears by late Sunday and continental high pressure \nbuilds back in. This will lead to generally fair weather with a risk \nfor frost or light freeze returning. Our next substantial chance for \nrain likely comes toward the end of next week (Thursday into \nFriday), following a mid week warm up that could see highs return \ninto the mid 70s F across the area by next Wednesday and especially \nThursday. /86/",
